# {{ .Vars.routines_dir }}/ — work that runs on a schedule

One document per scheduled thing. What it runs, what it may spend, who hears about it
when it breaks, and the date somebody has to decide it is still worth running.

This directory is the source. What actually runs it — a workflow, a crontab — is
generated from here by `ilk routine sync`, and `ilk check` reports the two disagreeing.

## Frontmatter

```yaml
---
id: dependency-audit          # what `ilk routine run` is given; unique here
title: Audit dependencies for published advisories
status: active                # active | paused
schedule: "0 4 * * 1"         # five cron fields, UTC
command: npm audit --audit-level=high
budget: 15                    # minutes, then it is killed
owner: platform               # a person or a team
review_by: 2027-02-08         # when somebody decides this still earns its keep
---
```

`@daily` and named months are not accepted, because the runners this is projected into
do not accept them either — a schedule that validates here and is ignored there is the
worst of both.

## Before adding one

Scheduled work is work nobody is watching. If a check, a hook or a gate could catch the
same problem when somebody causes it, that is strictly better — it fires while the fix
is cheap and the author still has the context. A schedule earns its place when the
trigger lives outside the repository and outside anybody's session: a vendor's
advisories, an expiring certificate, a report due on Monday.

The `write-a-routine` skill has the rest, including what GitHub's scheduler does and
does not promise.

## Commands

```sh
ilk routine list                      what runs, and what is overdue for review
ilk routine new "Audit dependencies"  write one
ilk routine run dependency-audit      run it here, under its budget
ilk routine sync                      project them into what actually runs them
```

Run a new routine by hand before committing it. One whose first real run is unattended
at 4am is one nobody has tested.
